{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-18963","model_call":12,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1728"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1728"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1298530399#8_2383172872","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1728","rank":5,"snippet":"claim. Many of those same accounts were also top superspreaders of the Dominion claim, and other voter fraud theories, on Twitter. The accounts of President Trump, his son Eric, Mr. Straka and Mr. Levin were all among the top 20 accounts that spread misinformation about voter fraud on Twitter, according to Ian Kennedy, a researcher at the University of Washington who works with the Elections Integrity Partnership. Mr. Trump had by far the largest influence on Twitter. A single tweet by the president accusing <cover>Dominion voting systems of deleting 2.7 million votes in his favor was shared over 185,000 times, and liked over 600,000 times. Like the other false claims about voter fraud, </cover>Mr. Trump’s tweet included a label by Twitter that he was sharing information that was not accurate. Twitter, like Facebook, has said that those labels help prevent false claims from being shared and direct people toward more authoritative sources of information.\" , \"start_char\": 5914.000000 , \"title\": \"How Misinformation ‘Superspreaders’ Seed False Election Theories - The New York Times\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nytimes.com/2020/11/23/"},"time":16},"model_calls":12,"document_judgments":11,"relevance_3":8,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
