{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-18963","model_call":24,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1731"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1731"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1226757922#11_2451618438","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1731","rank":4,"snippet":"will provide additional confidence that the votes were accurately counted.” Did Dominion Voting Software Delete 2.7 Million Ballots Cast for Donald Trump? No, there’s no evidence to support this claim and multiple election officials have disputed the accusations. While voter fraud claims concerning Dominion Voting Software started on the fringes on the internet, they officially went mainstream on Nov. 12, 2020 when U.S. President Donald Trump posted a message on Twitter claiming that <cover>Dominion had deleted 2.7 million votes that had cast for the incumbent. There is no truth to this claim. This rumor has even been disputed by government officials in Trump’s Department of Homeland Security. Shortly after Trump issued this tweet, a coalition of election security </cover>officials issued a joint statement saying that “there is no evidence that any voting system deleted or lost votes, changed votes, or was in any way compromised.” We took a deeper dive into this claim here .\" , \"start_char\": 6229.000000 , \"title\": \"RUMOR ALERT: Dominion Voting Systems Fraud Claims | Snopes.com\" , \"url\": \"https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/rumor-alert-","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1226757922#11_2451618438","rank":4,"relevance":3}]},"time":15},"model_calls":24,"document_judgments":23,"relevance_3":15,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
